// A2L question bank. Add a question by copying one line below.
// t = topic key from T, q = question, o = 4 options, a = index of the right option (0 = first),
// e = explanation, f:1 = show a CHECK flag (fact still to double-check).
// Other question types (set "type"):
//   multi: "select all that apply". a = list of right option indexes, e.g. a:[0,2,3].
//   match: tap a word, then its meaning. pairs = [[word, meaning], ...].
//   label: fill out a form by tapping a value, then a blank. slots = [[field, right value], ...]; extra = wrong values.
// R = where to read up on each topic (page numbers in the ESCO Low GWP Refrigerant Safety manual).
// A question can override it with its own r:"...".
const T={basics:"Safety class basics",fire:"Burning and limits",refs:"The refrigerants",rules:"EPA rules",detect:"Standards and leak detection",tools:"Tools and cylinders",haul:"Hauling and the truck",service:"Safe service",plate:"Reading the nameplate",leak:"Leaks, cold and oxygen",oil:"Oil, moisture and acid"};
const R={oil:"Lesson 2 on this site: What makes an A2L burn (section 3), and ESCO manual p. 21 (oils)",leak:"Guide on this site: Leaks: cold, oxygen and fire",plate:"Lesson 3 on this site: Read the A2L nameplate",basics:"ESCO manual pp. 21–24 (ASHRAE 34 safety classes) and p. 44 (burning velocity)",fire:"ESCO manual pp. 43–44 (combustion, HF, COF2, MIE, RCL and LFL)",refs:"ESCO manual pp. 13–21 (blends, glide, oils) and p. 53 (common A2Ls)",rules:"ESCO manual p. 6 (environmental rules) and pp. 52–53 (CFR 49, EPA SNAP)",detect:"ESCO manual p. 44 (LFL), p. 47 (mitigation) and p. 52 (standards summary)",tools:"ESCO manual pp. 38–39 (transport, storage), pp. 27–28 (recovery equipment), p. 37 (leak testing)",haul:"ESCO manual p. 3 and p. 38 (transportation) and p. 39 (storage)",service:"ESCO manual pp. 40–41 (jobsite safety, labels) and pp. 46–51 (A2L field procedures)"};
const Q=[
{t:"leak",q:"At normal air pressure, liquid R-410A boils at about what temperature?",o:["About -55°F (-48°C)","About 32°F (0°C)","About -10°F (-23°C)","About -150°F (-101°C)"],a:0,e:"R-410A boils at about -55°F at atmospheric pressure. Liquid spraying from a leak flashes to vapor at around that temperature, cold enough to frostbite skin or an eye in seconds. People pick 32°F because the frost on a leaking fitting looks like ice, but the refrigerant itself is far colder."},
{t:"leak",q:"Why does a big refrigerant leak in a small mechanical room become an oxygen hazard?",o:["The vapor is heavier than air, so it pools low and pushes the air (and oxygen) out","The refrigerant reacts with oxygen and uses it up","It's toxic like carbon monoxide","It rises and traps the oxygen at the ceiling"],a:0,e:"R-410A vapor is about 2.5 times heavier than air (about 72.6 vs 29 g/mol), and R-454B and R-32 are also heavier. It flows down and fills low spots, displacing air. It doesn't chemically burn up the oxygen and isn't toxic like CO. The danger is simply that it takes the air's place."},
{t:"leak",q:"OSHA calls air oxygen-deficient below what percent oxygen?",o:["19.5%","20.9%","16%","10%"],a:0,e:"Normal air is 20.9% oxygen. OSHA's line for oxygen-deficient is 19.5%. Around 16% and lower, judgment and coordination start to go. 20.9% is normal air, not the limit, and 16% is where symptoms start, not where it becomes unsafe."},
{t:"leak",q:"Rule of thumb: if refrigerant makes up 10% of the air in a room, about how much does the oxygen drop?",o:["About 2 points, from 20.9% to about 18.8%","About 10 points, to about 11%","It doesn't change","About 0.5 points"],a:0,e:"Air is about 21% oxygen, so whatever share the refrigerant takes, oxygen drops by about one fifth of that. Every 5% refrigerant lowers oxygen about 1 point. 10% refrigerant puts you near 18.8%, already under OSHA's 19.5% line. Picking 10 points is a common mistake: the refrigerant replaces air, and only a fifth of air is oxygen."},
{t:"leak",q:"Why don't you feel short of breath when a refrigerant leak lowers the oxygen?",o:["Your body's breathing alarm reacts to too much CO2, not too little oxygen","Refrigerant numbs your lungs","You do feel it right away, so you'll always have warning","Refrigerant adds oxygen as it warms up"],a:0,e:"The urge to breathe comes mostly from CO2 building up. In oxygen-poor air you still breathe out CO2 normally, so there's no gasping feeling. People just get dizzy or confused and can pass out. That's why you trust a detector or O2 monitor, not how you feel."},
{t:"leak",q:"For R-454B, which comes first as a leak builds up in a closed room?",o:["The air reaches the lower flammability limit while oxygen is still around 18%","Oxygen runs out long before the air could ever burn","Both happen at exactly the same level","R-454B can't reach its LFL indoors"],a:0,e:"R-454B's LFL is roughly 12% by volume. At that level oxygen is still around 18%, so you'd still be standing in air that could light off a pilot or a spark. That's why the RDS reacts at 25% of LFL, about 3%, well before either hazard.",f:1},
{t:"leak",q:"Refrigerant (A1 or A2L) hits a torch flame or a glowing heat strip. What does it break down into?",o:["Hydrogen fluoride and carbonyl fluoride, which are toxic and corrosive","Just CO2 and water","Nothing, A1 refrigerants can't break down","Chlorine gas only"],a:0,e:"Fluorinated refrigerants break down in flame or on hot surfaces into hydrogen fluoride (HF) and carbonyl fluoride. HF becomes hydrofluoric acid on anything wet, like your eyes and lungs. Being A1 doesn't protect you here. Clear refrigerant before brazing, and get out and ventilate if you smell something sharp."},
{t:"leak",q:"Which glove choice actually protects your hands from a liquid refrigerant spray?",o:["Insulated gloves rated for refrigerant, like neoprene or nitrile with a thermal liner","Thin disposable nitrile gloves","Cotton work gloves","Bare hands, so you can feel the fitting"],a:0,e:"Thin nitrile stops oil and dirt but not -55°F liquid. Cotton soaks up liquid refrigerant and holds it against your skin, which is worse. Use insulated, refrigerant-rated gloves, plus sealed goggles or a face shield for your eyes."},
{t:"leak",q:"You're about to crack a flare nut on a line that may still have refrigerant in it. Where should you stand?",o:["Off to the side of the fitting, never in line with it, with goggles on","Directly in front so you can see it clearly","Below the fitting, so the spray goes over you","It doesn't matter if you go slowly"],a:0,e:"A fitting that lets go sprays straight out. Stand to the side, keep your face out of the line and out of low spots where vapor pools, and wear goggles. Better yet, isolate or recover the refrigerant before you open anything."},
{t:"basics",q:"In the ASHRAE 34 safety class \"A2L\", what does the letter A mean?",o:["Lower toxicity","Approved for air conditioning","Higher toxicity","Azeotropic blend"],a:0,e:"ASHRAE 34 uses A for lower toxicity and B for higher toxicity. The number and letter after it (2L) describe flammability."},
{t:"basics",q:"What does \"2L\" mean in A2L?",o:["Lower flammability: it can burn, but the flame spreads slowly","No flame spread at all","Highly flammable, like propane","It has two liquid phases"],a:0,e:"Class 2L is lower flammability. It is part of class 2 (flammable) with a maximum burning velocity of 10 cm/s or less. Class 1 shows no flame spread, and class 3 (propane, R-290) is highly flammable."},
{t:"basics",q:"What is the maximum burning velocity for a refrigerant to be classed 2L?",o:["10 cm/s (about 3.9 in/s)","1 m/s","50 cm/s","There is no limit"],a:0,e:"To be 2L the refrigerant's maximum burning velocity must be 10 cm/s or less. That slow flame is why A2Ls are hard to ignite and burn weakly, but they still can burn."},
{t:"basics",q:"What ASHRAE safety class is R-410A?",o:["A1","A2L","A3","B1"],a:0,e:"R-410A is A1: lower toxicity and no flame spread. That is the big change with R-32 and R-454B, which are A2L."},
{t:"basics",q:"If an A2L refrigerant does burn, what dangerous gas can it make?",o:["Hydrogen fluoride (HF)","Carbon dioxide only","Ozone","Nothing harmful"],a:0,e:"Burning fluorinated refrigerants (A1 or A2L) can make hydrogen fluoride, which is toxic and corrosive. Ventilate, and never breathe the smoke from a refrigerant fire or a brazing burn-off."},
{t:"refs",q:"Which of these is an A2L refrigerant?",o:["R-454B","R-410A","R-22","R-134a"],a:0,e:"R-454B is A2L. R-410A, R-22 and R-134a are all A1. Other common A2Ls: R-32, R-454C, R-452B, R-1234yf and R-1234ze(E)."},
{t:"refs",q:"R-454B is a blend of which two refrigerants?",o:["R-32 and R-1234yf","R-32 and R-125","R-125 and R-134a","R-290 and R-600a"],a:0,e:"R-454B is about 68.9% R-32 and 31.1% R-1234yf by weight. R-410A, for comparison, is R-32 and R-125."},
{t:"refs",q:"R-32 is:",o:["A single-component refrigerant with no glide","A blend with a large glide","A natural refrigerant","An A1 refrigerant"],a:0,e:"R-32 is a single compound (difluoromethane), so it has no temperature glide. It is also one of the two parts of R-410A and R-454B."},
{t:"refs",q:"How should you charge R-454B into a system?",o:["As a liquid, weighed in","As a vapor so it evaporates evenly","Either way, it does not matter","Only by superheat with no scale"],a:0,e:"R-454B is a zeotropic blend with a small glide, so charge it as liquid to keep the blend right. Weigh it in with a scale to the nameplate charge, then check with the maker's charging method."},
{t:"refs",q:"Can you top off an R-410A system with R-454B?",o:["No. Never mix refrigerants, and R-454B is not a drop-in for R-410A","Yes, if pressures match","Yes, up to 20% of the charge","Only in heating mode"],a:0,e:"Never mix refrigerants. R-454B equipment is built and listed for A2L (sensors, labels, charge limits). Putting A2L into an R-410A system is not allowed."},
{t:"refs",q:"Compared to R-410A, R-454B operating pressures are:",o:["Close to R-410A (slightly lower)","About half","About double","The same as R-22"],a:0,e:"R-454B runs close to R-410A, a little lower. R-32 runs a little higher than R-410A. Use the right P/T chart, or a digital gauge set to the right refrigerant.",f:1},
{t:"refs",q:"About what is the GWP of R-410A, and of R-454B?",o:["About 2,088 and about 466","About 675 and about 2,088","About 1 and about 4","Both under 150"],a:0,e:"R-410A is about 2,088 (AR4). R-454B is about 466 and R-32 about 675. That is why they fit under the EPA 700 GWP limit for new AC and heat pumps.",f:1},
{t:"rules",q:"Which law lets EPA phase down HFC refrigerants like R-410A?",o:["The AIM Act of 2020","The Montreal Protocol only","OSHA 1910","The Energy Policy Act of 1992"],a:0,e:"The American Innovation and Manufacturing (AIM) Act of 2020 gives EPA authority to phase down HFC production and import by 85% by 2036 and to set GWP limits on new equipment."},
{t:"rules",q:"Under EPA's Technology Transitions rule, new residential and light commercial AC and heat pump systems made or imported from January 1, 2025 must use a refrigerant with a GWP below:",o:["700","150","2,000","10"],a:0,e:"The limit is 700 GWP. That is why new split systems moved to R-454B and R-32. Check the current EPA rule, as EPA has changed some dates since 2023.",f:1},
{t:"rules",q:"In 2026 EPA changed the Technology Transitions rule for residential and light commercial AC. What changed?",o:["It removed the Jan 1, 2026 install deadline for R-410A equipment made or imported before 2025","It banned all R-410A service work","It made R-454B illegal","It ended Section 608 certification"],a:0,e:"In May 2026 EPA finalized a change removing the Jan 1, 2026 install deadline for systems made or imported before Jan 1, 2025. Leftover R-410A stock can be installed until it runs out. Servicing existing R-410A systems was never banned.",f:1},
{t:"rules",q:"Do you still need EPA Section 608 certification to work on A2L systems?",o:["Yes. 608 still applies to buying and handling these refrigerants","No, A2Ls are exempt","Only for systems over 50 lb","Only for R-32"],a:0,e:"Section 608 applies. EPA does not issue a separate A2L certificate, but A2L safety training is strongly recommended and some employers and makers require it.",f:1},
{t:"rules",q:"Can you vent R-32 or R-454B because they have a low GWP?",o:["No. Venting is illegal; recover it","Yes, small amounts are fine","Yes, outdoors only","Only R-32"],a:0,e:"Do not vent HFC refrigerants. Recover with equipment rated for A2L. Low GWP does not make venting legal."},
{t:"detect",q:"Which standard covers how refrigerants are named and given safety classes like A2L?",o:["ASHRAE 34","ASHRAE 62.1","UL 1995","NFPA 70"],a:0,e:"ASHRAE 34 assigns the R-numbers and safety groups. ASHRAE 15 (and 15.2 for homes) is the safety standard for refrigeration systems in buildings. UL 60335-2-40 is the product safety standard for heat pumps, AC and dehumidifiers."},
{t:"detect",q:"UL 60335-2-40 is the product safety standard for:",o:["Electric heat pumps, air conditioners and dehumidifiers","Refrigerant recovery cylinders","Brazing alloys","Ductwork"],a:0,e:"UL 60335-2-40 is the safety standard that A2L equipment is listed to. It sets things like charge limits by room size and when a refrigerant detection system is needed."},
{t:"detect",q:"Most A2L comfort equipment has a refrigerant detection system (RDS). At what level does it usually call for mitigation?",o:["25% of the lower flammability limit (LFL)","100% of LFL","Any trace at all","Only when the flame sensor trips"],a:0,e:"The RDS typically triggers mitigation at a concentration of 25% of LFL or more, well before the air could burn. Follow the maker's instructions for the exact setup.",f:1},
{t:"detect",q:"After an RDS detects a leak, how fast must it call for mitigation, and how long does the signal stay on?",o:["Within 15 seconds, and on for at least 5 minutes","Within 5 minutes, and on for 15 seconds","Within 1 hour, until reset","No time limit"],a:0,e:"Per UL guidance on UL 60335-2-40, the RDS must call for mitigation within 15 seconds and keep the signal for at least 5 minutes before reset.",f:1},
{t:"detect",q:"Which is a typical mitigation action when the RDS detects a leak?",o:["Run the indoor blower to mix the air","Turn on the electric heat strips","Close all zone dampers","Start the compressor"],a:0,e:"Typical actions: run the indoor fan, open zone dampers fully, shut off the compressor, de-energize ignition sources like electric heat, and close shutoff valves on multi-coil systems like VRF."},
{t:"detect",q:"Where are A2L leak sensors usually placed, and why?",o:["Low, near the bottom of the indoor coil, because A2Ls are heavier than air","At the ceiling, because A2Ls rise","At the outdoor unit only","In the return grille"],a:0,e:"A2L vapor is heavier than air, so a leak settles low. Sensors sit low near the coil. When sniffing for leaks, check low too."},
{t:"detect",q:"Roughly what charge size triggers a built-in refrigerant detection system for most fixed A2L equipment?",o:["Over about 4 lb (about 1.8 kg)","Over 50 lb","Over 0.1 lb","Never required"],a:0,e:"UL describes an RDS as generally expected over about 4 lb for most equipment (and over about 2 lb for non-fixed factory-sealed units). So almost all A2L split systems and VRF have one. The exact rule is in UL 60335-2-40 and the install manual.",f:1},
{t:"tools",q:"What marks a refrigerant cylinder as A2L?",o:["A red band at the top of a gray cylinder","A solid green cylinder","A yellow top","A blue band"],a:0,e:"Cylinders are now gray with the R-number printed on them. A2L cylinders have a red band around the top for mild flammability."},
{t:"tools",q:"Why might your old hose not thread onto a new R-32 or R-454B cylinder?",o:["Most A2L cylinders use a left-hand thread valve (CGA 164)","They use metric pipe thread","They have no valve","They need a flare nut"],a:0,e:"Most makers switched to a left-hand thread (CGA 164) on A2L cylinders, following an AHRI guideline. Get the right adapter from your supply house."},
{t:"tools",q:"What recovery machine and vacuum pump should you use on A2L systems?",o:["Ones rated for A2L (spark-resistant)","Any machine that works on R-410A","Any shop vac","None, A2Ls cannot be recovered"],a:0,e:"Use recovery machines and vacuum pumps rated for A2L. They are built to avoid sparks from switches and motors. Recovery cylinders must also be rated for flammable refrigerant."},
{t:"tools",q:"What kind of leak detector should you use on R-454B?",o:["One rated for A2L or that lists R-454B","Any old R-22 detector","A match flame","Bubbles only, never electronic"],a:0,e:"Use a detector rated for the refrigerant. Some older heated-diode or flame-type detectors are not safe or not sensitive to A2Ls. Bubbles are still a good way to pinpoint."},
{t:"service",q:"Before brazing on an A2L system you should:",o:["Recover the charge, evacuate, purge with nitrogen, then braze with nitrogen flowing","Braze fast with the charge in","Just open the service valves","Pressurize with air"],a:0,e:"Remove the refrigerant first, pull a vacuum, purge the lines with dry nitrogen, and keep nitrogen flowing while brazing. Never braze with refrigerant in the lines."},
{t:"service",q:"Before you start work around an A2L unit, what is a good first step?",o:["Check the area with a leak detector and make sure there is ventilation","Light a torch to test for gas","Turn off the indoor fan","Close the doors to keep air still"],a:0,e:"Check for refrigerant in the work area first, keep it ventilated, and keep ignition sources away. This is the same idea used in A2L safety training."},
{t:"service",q:"How is the maximum A2L charge for an indoor space usually limited?",o:["By the room's floor area (minimum room area)","By the tonnage only","By outdoor temperature","It is not limited"],a:0,e:"UL 60335-2-40 and ASHRAE 15 tie the charge to the size of the room it serves (minimum room area). Bigger charges need a bigger room or added safety measures. The install manual has the table for the unit."},
{t:"service",q:"For shipping in the US, R-32 and R-454B cylinders are what DOT hazard class?",o:["Class 2.1 flammable gas","Class 2.2 non-flammable gas","Not regulated","Class 8 corrosive"],a:0,e:"R-32 and R-454B ship as Division 2.1 flammable gas, so they need the flammable gas label. R-410A ships as 2.2 non-flammable. Check DOT rules for how much you can carry in your truck.",f:1},
{t:"fire",q:"Besides hydrogen fluoride (HF), what other toxic gas can form when a fluorinated refrigerant burns or breaks down from heat?",o:["Carbonyl fluoride (COF2)","Nitrogen","Argon","Water vapor only"],a:0,e:"Thermal breakdown can make carbonyl fluoride (COF2) as well as HF. Both are toxic. A sharp, acrid smell near a flame or hot surface is a warning sign: ventilate and get out of the area."},
{t:"fire",q:"What does minimum ignition energy (MIE) mean?",o:["The smallest spark energy that can light the refrigerant-air mix","The heat the compressor makes at startup","The voltage needed to run the RDS","The energy needed to recover refrigerant"],a:0,e:"MIE is the smallest amount of spark energy that can ignite the mixture. A2Ls have a much higher MIE than propane (A3), which is one reason they are harder to ignite. A hard-to-light gas can still be lit by a strong enough source."},
{t:"fire",q:"Heat of combustion (HOC) tells you:",o:["How much heat the refrigerant gives off when it burns","How hot the discharge line gets","The boiling point at sea level","How fast the flame spreads"],a:0,e:"Heat of combustion is how much heat is released when the refrigerant burns. ASHRAE 34 uses it with LFL to sort class 2 from class 3. Flame speed is a separate measure, burning velocity."},
{t:"fire",q:"What is the lower flammability limit (LFL)?",o:["The lowest concentration in air that can burn","The lowest pressure the system can run at","The lowest temperature the refrigerant can boil","The smallest leak a detector can find"],a:0,e:"Below the LFL, there is too little refrigerant in the air to burn. Leak sensors trip at 25% of LFL so action happens long before the air could burn."},
{t:"fire",q:"How does the refrigerant concentration limit (RCL) relate to the LFL?",o:["RCL is a safety limit set well below the LFL for occupied spaces","RCL is always higher than LFL","They are the same number","RCL only applies to A1 refrigerants"],a:0,e:"ASHRAE 34 sets the RCL as the most refrigerant allowed in an occupied space. It considers toxicity, lack of oxygen and flammability. For A2Ls the flammability part is set at a fraction of the LFL, so RCL sits well below it.",f:1},
{t:"service",q:"What gas should you use to pressure test an A2L system for leaks?",o:["Dry nitrogen","Oxygen","Compressed shop air","The A2L refrigerant itself at full pressure"],a:0,e:"Pressure test with dry nitrogen to the maker's test pressure. Never use oxygen, which can explode with oil. Never use compressed air, which brings in moisture and oxygen."},
{t:"service",q:"When you make refrigerant joints inside the occupied space on an A2L system, what kind of joints are expected?",o:["Permanent joints, like brazed or listed press fittings, per the maker's instructions","Any reused flare nut","Taped threaded fittings","Compression fittings from the plumbing aisle"],a:0,e:"Codes and UL 60335-2-40 push for permanent joints indoors, such as brazed or listed mechanical/press fittings. Reusing old flare connections indoors is restricted. Follow the install manual for what that unit allows.",f:1},
{t:"tools",q:"How should you store A2L refrigerant cylinders?",o:["Upright, secured, ventilated, away from heat and ignition sources","Lying in the sun on the truck dash","Next to the water heater","In a sealed closet with no airflow"],a:0,e:"Store cylinders upright and secured, in a ventilated spot, away from heat and anything that can spark. Keep them out of high heat; most guidance says never above 125°F (52°C)."},
{t:"tools",q:"How full can you fill a recovery cylinder?",o:["No more than 80% full of liquid","100%, to save trips","50% maximum","As full as the scale allows"],a:0,e:"Never fill a recovery cylinder past 80% liquid. It needs room for the liquid to expand as it warms. This is the same rule from your 608, and it matters more when the gas can burn."},
{t:"refs",q:"Which oils are common in HFC and A2L systems like R-410A, R-454B and R-32?",o:["POE and PVE","Mineral oil only","Alkylbenzene only","Motor oil"],a:0,e:"Most of these systems use POE (polyol ester) oil, and some, including some VRF, use PVE (polyvinyl ether). Mineral oil does not mix well with HFCs. Always use the oil type the compressor maker lists."},
{t:"refs",q:"Why do you keep POE oil containers sealed?",o:["POE absorbs moisture from the air","POE is flammable at room temperature","POE turns into refrigerant","It is just to keep it clean"],a:0,e:"POE is hygroscopic, meaning it pulls moisture from the air. Wet oil leads to acid and system problems. Keep containers sealed and only open them when you need them."},
{t:"rules",q:"What is EPA's SNAP program?",o:["EPA's list of which refrigerants are acceptable to use, and in which equipment","A refrigerant brand","A leak detection method","An OSHA safety course"],a:0,e:"SNAP (Significant New Alternatives Policy) is EPA's review of substitutes for ozone-depleting refrigerants. It lists which ones are acceptable in which uses, sometimes with use conditions such as following UL standards."},
{t:"detect",q:"UL 60335-2-89 covers which kind of equipment?",o:["Commercial refrigeration, like display cases and ice machines","Home split system AC","Refrigerant cylinders","Brazing torches"],a:0,e:"UL 60335-2-89 is the safety standard for commercial refrigerating appliances. Comfort heat pumps and AC fall under UL 60335-2-40."},
{t:"rules",q:"Which federal regulations cover transporting refrigerant cylinders, like in your work truck?",o:["CFR Title 49 (DOT)","CFR Title 29 (OSHA)","ASHRAE 15","UL 60335-2-40"],a:0,e:"CFR Title 49 is the DOT's hazardous materials transport rules. That is where flammable gas (2.1) shipping, labeling and quantity limits come from."},
{t:"service",q:"What A2L marking should you expect on new equipment?",o:["A flammable refrigerant warning label","No label, A2Ls are not flammable","Only a GWP sticker","A green A1 sticker"],a:0,e:"A2L equipment carries flammable refrigerant warning labels and markings near access panels and service points. If you see one, follow A2L procedures, even if it looks like an ordinary unit."},
{t:"service",type:"multi",q:"You're about to open up an A2L system in a small mechanical room. Which of these are hazards you need to deal with before you start? Select all that apply.",o:["A gas water heater with a standing pilot light","Electric heat strips that are still energized","A coworker smoking by the door","A closed room with no airflow","Your A2L-rated recovery machine","A sealed cylinder strapped upright to a cart"],a:[0,1,2,3],e:"Before work starts, look for anything that can light a leak (pilot lights, energized heat strips, smoking, sparking tools) and for still air where vapor can build up. A2L vapor is heavier than air, so it collects low, right where pilot lights sit. Check the area with your detector and get air moving. A rated recovery machine and a closed, strapped cylinder are not hazards.",r:"ESCO manual p. 40 (jobsite safety)"},
{t:"haul",type:"multi",q:"Which of these are good practice when hauling A2L cylinders in your truck? Select all that apply.",o:["Stand them upright and strap them down","Ride them in the bed or a vented cargo area, not the cab","Keep valve caps or guards on","Keep them out of direct sun and away from heat","Lay them on the cab floor with the AC running","Crack the valve open a little to let pressure off"],a:[0,1,2,3],e:"Upright and secured so they can't roll or tip, valves protected, out of the cab, and away from heat. Never crack a valve to vent pressure. That releases flammable gas inside your vehicle.",f:1},
{t:"haul",q:"At the supply house, what should you check before you load an A2L cylinder into your truck?",o:["That it's the right refrigerant, the valve is closed and capped, and the cylinder isn't damaged or leaking","Crack the valve to make sure it's full","Nothing, the supply house already checked it","Turn it upside down to check for liquid"],a:0,e:"Check the label and the red band for the right refrigerant, make sure the valve is shut with the cap on, and look for dents, rust or a hiss. Then load it upright and strap it down. A leaking cylinder doesn't go in your truck."},
{t:"haul",q:"You're driving and hear hissing from a refrigerant cylinder in your van. What do you do first?",o:["Pull over safely, shut off the engine, get out, and open the doors to air it out","Keep driving with the windows down to the job","Turn the heater fan on high to blow it around","Keep the doors shut so it can't spread"],a:0,e:"Stop ignition sources: pull over and shut off the engine. Get out and ventilate. A2L vapor is heavier than air and settles on the floor of the van. If it's safe, close the valve with gloves on (liquid can freeze skin). Don't restart or light anything until it clears."},
{t:"haul",q:"Does DOT require a refrigerant leak alarm in a service truck that carries A2L cylinders?",o:["No. DOT rules are about securing, labeling and limiting what you carry, but a portable A2L detector is smart to keep on board","Yes, every truck needs a hard-wired alarm","Only for R-32","Only when you carry more than 50 lb"],a:0,e:"DOT (49 CFR) covers how flammable gas cylinders are packaged, marked, secured and how much you can carry. As of 2026 I don't know of any DOT rule that requires a leak alarm in the truck. Your company or local rules may ask for more. A portable A2L detector in the truck is cheap insurance.",f:1},
{t:"basics",q:"What's the big difference between an A3 refrigerant like R-290 (propane) and an A2L like R-454B?",o:["A3 lights easily and burns fast. A2L is hard to light and the flame is slow (10 cm/s or less)","A3 is more toxic","A2L can't burn at all","There's no real difference"],a:0,e:"Both are lower toxicity (the A). The number is the flammability: 3 means highly flammable, like propane. 2L means lower flammability: it needs much more spark energy to light, and the flame crawls. That's why A3 systems keep charges very small."},
{t:"basics",type:"match",q:"Match each safety class to its example.",pairs:[["A1","R-410A: lower toxicity, no flame spread"],["A2L","R-454B: lower toxicity, lower flammability"],["A3","R-290 (propane): lower toxicity, highly flammable"],["B2L","R-717 (ammonia): higher toxicity, lower flammability"]],e:"The letter is toxicity (A lower, B higher). The number is flammability (1 none, 2L lower, 3 high). Ammonia is the classic B2L."},
{t:"fire",type:"match",q:"Match each term to what it means.",pairs:[["LFL","The lowest amount in the air that can burn"],["MIE","The smallest spark that can light the mix"],["Burning velocity","How fast the flame spreads"],["HOC","How much heat comes off when it burns"],["RDS","The leak sensor system built into the unit"]],e:"These letters show up all over A2L material. LFL and MIE tell you how hard it is to light. Burning velocity and HOC tell you how hard it burns. RDS is the refrigerant detection system in the equipment."},
{t:"service",type:"label",q:"You just finished an A2L install. Fill out the system label. Tap a value, then tap the blank it goes in.",slots:[["Company name","Smith Heating & Air"],["Evacuation level","450 microns"],["Leak test pressure","400 psig dry nitrogen"],["Test date","10/27/2026"],["Refrigerant type","R-454B"],["Total charge","9 lb 4 oz"]],extra:["Customer's phone number","R-410A","Compressor serial number"],e:"UL standards require the installing tech to fill out this label with a permanent marker: who installed it, how deep the vacuum got, what pressure it was leak tested at, the date, the refrigerant, and the total charge in pounds and ounces. Honestly, it's good practice on every system, not just A2L. The next tech will thank you.",r:"ESCO manual p. 41 (labeling and marking)"},
{t:"plate",q:"A rooftop unit's nameplate says R-454B, Circuit 1: 288 oz, Circuit 2: 288 oz. What's the total charge?",o:["36 lb","18 lb","28.8 lb","576 lb"],a:0,e:"Each circuit is its own system. 288 oz ÷ 16 = 18 lb per circuit, times 2 circuits = 36 lb. 18 lb is the mistake of reading only one circuit. 28.8 lb comes from dividing by 10 instead of 16."},
{t:"plate",q:"The nameplate lists \"UL 60335-2-40\". What does that tell you?",o:["The equipment is built and listed to the safety standard that covers A2L heat pumps and AC","It's a building code for the room","It's the EPA rule for refrigerant sales","It's the DOT rule for shipping the unit"],a:0,e:"UL 60335-2-40 is the equipment safety standard. On an A2L unit it covers things like the leak sensor, ignition sources inside the unit and the markings. The room rules come from ASHRAE 15 and your local code."},
{t:"plate",q:"The nameplate shows \"Minimum room area: 25.0 m² (269 ft²)\". What does that mean?",o:["The unit can't run or be stored in a space smaller than that, based on how much refrigerant could leak","It's the smallest room the unit can cool","It's how much roof space the unit needs","It's the clearance around the unit"],a:0,e:"Minimum room area comes from the charge and the refrigerant's LFL. If the whole charge leaked into a smaller room, it could reach a level that burns. Clearances are a separate line on the plate.",f:1},
{t:"plate",q:"A plate says \"Max refrigerant pressure: Low side 450 psig, High side 600 psig\". You're pressure testing after a repair. What's the rule?",o:["Don't go over the low-side rating on parts that see low-side pressure","Test everything to 600 psig, the higher number","Test to 150% of 600 psig to be safe","Pressure ratings don't matter for nitrogen"],a:0,e:"Nitrogen at high pressure can damage low-side parts. Stay at or under the rating for the side you're testing, and follow the maker's test pressure. 600 psig is the high-side limit only.",f:1},
{t:"plate",q:"What does the black flame symbol in a diamond with \"Safety group A2L\" mean on a nameplate?",o:["Flammable refrigerant inside. Follow A2L service steps","The unit has a gas furnace","Hot surface, don't touch","Electrical shock hazard"],a:0,e:"That symbol warns that the refrigerant is flammable. A unit can have both gas heat and A2L refrigerant, so don't assume the flame means only the furnace."},
{t:"plate",q:"The disconnect on a new rooftop unit is a molded case switch. Its label says \"provides no overcurrent protection\". What does that mean?",o:["It's only a switch. The breaker or fuses upstream must protect the unit, sized to the nameplate's max overcurrent protection","It works like a breaker and protects the unit","You can skip the breaker in the panel","It's only for 120 V circuits"],a:0,e:"A molded case switch looks like a breaker but doesn't trip on overload. Protection has to come from the breaker or fuses feeding the unit, no bigger than the nameplate's max overcurrent protection (MOCP)."},
{t:"service",q:"What do m1 and m2 mean on an A2L system?",o:["m1 is the most charge allowed with no mitigation (no sensor and fan); m2 is the higher limit allowed when mitigation is installed","m1 is the factory charge and m2 is the field-added charge for long lines","m1 is the charge in summer and m2 is the charge in winter","m1 is the charge for the outdoor unit and m2 for the indoor coil"],a:0,e:"Both are charge limits from the A2L safety standards. With no mitigation, you have to stay at or under the lower limit (m1). With mitigation, like a leak sensor that starts the fan, a bigger charge can be allowed (m2), still sized to the room. People mix it up with factory vs field charge because both show up on the nameplate and in the install manual.",r:"ESCO manual p. 50 (system charging) and UL 60335-2-40"},
{t:"service",type:"multi",q:"Which of these change how much A2L refrigerant a system is allowed to hold? Select all that apply.",o:["The system size in Btu/h","Line set length and size","The indoor room volume in cubic feet","What the building is used for (occupancy)","The outdoor temperature on the day you charge"],a:[0,1,2,3],e:"The allowed charge is set by the equipment, the piping, how big the room is, and how the building is used. Outdoor temp changes your pressures and how you charge, but it doesn't change the safety limit. Always check the maker's charge info for that model.",r:"ESCO manual p. 50 (system charging)"},
{t:"service",q:"A customer's R-454B system is a little low and you only have R-32 on the truck. Both are A2L. Can you top it off with R-32?",o:["No. Different refrigerants can't be mixed, even in the same safety class","Yes, because they are both A2L","Yes, as long as you add less than 10% of the charge","Yes, because R-454B is mostly R-32 anyway"],a:0,e:"Same safety class doesn't mean same refrigerant. Mixing makes a blend nobody has a PT chart for, so the system runs erratic and you can damage parts. The \"mostly R-32\" answer is a real trap: R-454B is about 69% R-32, but the rest is R-1234yf, and that changes everything.",r:"ESCO manual p. 50 (system charging)"},
{t:"service",q:"You recovered R-454B from a unit. Without sending it off to be reclaimed, where can it go?",o:["Back into the same system, or another system with the same owner","Into any R-454B system on your next call","Only into a new recovery cylinder to sell","Nowhere. Recovered A2L must always be destroyed"],a:0,e:"Recovered refrigerant that hasn't been reclaimed can go back into the system it came from, or another system owned by the same owner. To be sold for other customers, it has to be reclaimed to the AHRI 700 purity standard first. Techs pick \"any R-454B system\" because it seems like the same gas, but you don't know what's in it.",r:"ESCO manual p. 50, EPA Section 608 rules"},
{t:"service",q:"What deep vacuum target do most techs and many makers use before charging an A2L system?",o:["500 microns, unless the maker calls for something different","5,000 microns","29 inches of mercury on the compound gauge","0 psig"],a:0,e:"500 microns is the common target. Some makers want a different number, and some want triple evacuation (pull a vacuum, break it with dry nitrogen, repeat 3 times). The compound gauge can't read a deep vacuum, so \"29 inHg\" is a common mistake. Use a micron gauge.",r:"ESCO manual p. 50 (evacuation)"},
{t:"tools",q:"Your vacuum pump is running on an A2L system in a small indoor mechanical room. What might the local code ask for?",o:["Pipe the pump's exhaust outdoors, or run mechanical ventilation that brings in fresh air","Nothing. Vacuum pumps don't let out refrigerant","Put the pump in a closed cabinet","Run the pump only at night"],a:0,e:"Some code officials want the pump exhaust piped outside, or fresh air brought in, during evacuation. That's mostly for indoor equipment rooms and commercial kitchens. Thinking \"the pump doesn't release refrigerant\" is the trap: whatever is left in the system comes out the exhaust.",r:"ESCO manual p. 50 (evacuation)",f:1},
{t:"service",q:"What does \"intrinsically safe\" mean on A2L equipment and tools?",o:["It can't put out enough spark or heat to light the refrigerant, even if something fails","It has a thermal fuse that shuts it off when it gets hot","It is waterproof and safe to use in the rain","It is rated for 600 volts"],a:0,e:"Intrinsically safe parts can't release enough electrical or heat energy to ignite the gas, in normal use or when something goes wrong. Parts like contactors on A2L units are sealed so a spark stays inside the enclosure. Techs mix it up with a thermal fuse or a high voltage rating, but neither one stops a spark from reaching the gas.",r:"ESCO manual p. 51 (system repairs)"},
{t:"service",q:"You need to discharge a run capacitor on an A2L unit. What's the safe way?",o:["Use a resistor or a capacitor discharge tool so it bleeds off without a spark","Short across the terminals with an insulated screwdriver","Pull the wires off and let them touch","Wait 10 seconds, then grab it"],a:0,e:"Shorting a cap with a screwdriver is how a lot of us learned, but it makes a spark, and that's an ignition source if refrigerant has leaked. Use a resistor or discharge tool, and verify it's at zero with your meter.",r:"ESCO manual p. 51 (system repairs)"},
{t:"service",q:"The contactor on an A2L rooftop is burnt. The supply house only has a generic one. What do you do?",o:["Use the maker's specified (OEM) part. A generic one may not be sealed and could spark into a leak","Install the generic one if the coil voltage and amps match","Install it and remove the cover so it runs cooler","Jumper the contactor until the part comes in"],a:0,e:"On A2L units the contactor is part of the ignition protection. A generic part with matching volts and amps can still have open contacts that spark into leaked refrigerant. Use the part the maker lists, put the enclosure back together right, and replace worn seals.",r:"ESCO manual p. 51 (system repairs)"},
{t:"service",type:"multi",q:"Before you troubleshoot live on an A2L unit, which of these should you do? Select all that apply.",o:["Make sure the unit is properly grounded","Check for leaked refrigerant in the area first","Only work live on intrinsically safe parts if gas could be present","Reseal any enclosure you open before putting the unit back in service","Prop panels open so the parts cool off"],a:[0,1,2,3],e:"Ground first, check the air, and only work live on intrinsically safe parts where gas could be present. When you're done, close up the sealed enclosures properly and check everything before you put it back in service. Leaving panels open defeats the sealing that keeps a spark away from the gas.",r:"ESCO manual p. 51 (system repairs)"},
{t:"service",q:"An A2L unit's charge label shows refrigerant added three times in the last few years. What should you think?",o:["There's a leak. Find it and fix it before you add more","It's normal for A2L systems to lose a little each year","The first tech undercharged it","The label is wrong. A2L systems don't need labels"],a:0,e:"A pattern of top-offs means a leak. Find it, repair it, and pressure test before you recharge. Every charge, the first one and every one after, goes on the unit's label with the amount and the date, and the service record stays with the equipment.",r:"ESCO manual p. 51 (charge records)",f:1},
{t:"plate",q:"A unit's nameplate says it was made in 2025 and uses R-454B. Why R-454B instead of R-410A?",o:["New home and light commercial AC made from 2025 must use a refrigerant under 700 GWP","R-410A was banned from all use in 2025","R-454B is cheaper","R-454B runs at much lower pressure"],a:0,e:"Under EPA's Technology Transitions rule, new systems made from Jan 1, 2025 have to be under 700 GWP. R-410A is about 2,088, and R-454B is about 466. Servicing existing R-410A systems is still allowed.",f:1},
{t:"plate",type:"multi",q:"You open the side of a new rooftop unit. Which of these tell you it holds an A2L refrigerant? Select all that apply.",o:["Red tags with a flame symbol on the piping","A pink diamond label that says FLAMMABLE GAS 2","A sticker that says R-32 REFRIGERANT","A \"HOT PIPING\" caution label","A \"Refrigerant under pressure\" warning"],a:[0,1,2],e:"The red flame tags, the DOT flammable gas diamond and the R-32 sticker all point to a flammable refrigerant. Hot-piping and pressure warnings are on every unit, A1 or A2L, so they don't tell you anything about the refrigerant.",r:"Lesson 3 on this site: Spot the A2L markings"},
{t:"rules",type:"match",q:"Match each year to the rule that goes with it.",pairs:[["2020","AIM Act passes: EPA starts the HFC phase-down"],["2025","New home and light commercial AC must be under 700 GWP"],["May 2026","Leftover R-410A units made before 2025 can still be installed"],["2036","HFC production down 85%"]],e:"Dates as of Oct 2026. The AIM Act (2020) started the phase-down. Equipment made from Jan 1, 2025 has to be under 700 GWP. In May 2026 EPA removed the install deadline for leftover R-410A units made before 2025. The phase-down reaches 85% by 2036.",f:1,r:"Lesson 3 on this site: Match the year to the rule. Cheat sheet: Dates that matter"},
{t:"basics",q:"Ammonia (R-717) is class B2L. What does the B tell you?",o:["Higher toxicity: the exposure limit is under 400 ppm","It burns faster than an A refrigerant","It's a blend","It's banned in buildings"],a:0,e:"The letter is toxicity. A means the occupational exposure limit is 400 ppm or more, B means it's under 400 ppm. The number after it (2L) is how it burns. People guess 'burns faster' because they mix up the letter and the number."},
{t:"basics",type:"multi",q:"Which of these are real ASHRAE 34 safety classes? Select all that apply.",o:["A1","A2L","B2L","B3","A4","C1"],a:[0,1,2,3],e:"There are eight: A1, A2L, A2, A3, B1, B2L, B2 and B3. Toxicity only goes A or B, and flammability only goes 1, 2L, 2 or 3. There's no class 4 and no C."},
{t:"basics",q:"Use the rule of 90 on R-32. What do you get?",o:["122: 1 carbon, 2 hydrogen, 2 fluorine","32: 3 carbon, 2 fluorine","122: 1 chlorine, 2 hydrogen, 2 fluorine","It's a blend, so the rule doesn't work"],a:0,e:"Add 90 to the number: 32 + 90 = 122. The digits are carbon, hydrogen, fluorine. R-32 is CH2F2 (difluoromethane). R-32 is a single compound, not a blend. It's an ingredient in blends like R-410A and R-454B."},
{t:"basics",q:"What does the capital B in R-454B mean?",o:["It's one recipe of that blend. R-454A, B and C use the same refrigerants in different amounts","It's higher toxicity, like the B in B2L","It's the backup refrigerant for R-454A","It stands for blend"],a:0,e:"After a 400- or 500-series number, the capital letter is the recipe version. R-454B is about 69% R-32 and 31% R-1234yf. The B in a safety class like B2L is a different thing entirely. That mix-up is easy to make on a test."},
{t:"basics",type:"match",q:"Match each R-number series to what it is.",pairs:[["R-4xx","Zeotropic blend with glide, like R-410A and R-454B"],["R-5xx","Azeotropic blend that acts like one refrigerant"],["R-7xx","Inorganic, like ammonia R-717 and CO2 R-744"],["R-1xxx","Has a double bond (HFO), like R-1234yf"]],e:"The 400s are zeotropic blends, the 500s azeotropes, the 600s organics like isobutane, and the 700s inorganics (700 plus the molecule's weight: ammonia 17, CO2 44). Four-digit numbers starting with 1 have a double bond, which is why HFOs have such a low GWP."},
{t:"basics",q:"A spec sheet says HFC-134a. The cylinder says R-134a. Are they the same?",o:["Yes. The prefix only tells you the chemical family","No, HFC-134a is a newer blend","No, HFC means it's flammable","Only if the cylinder has a red band"],a:0,e:"Same refrigerant. ASHRAE 34 allows a prefix like CFC, HCFC, HFC or HFO in place of the R to show what's in it. R-22 is HCFC-22. It's the same molecule either way."},
{t:"basics",q:"Why should a tech care about a refrigerant's safety class if you just charge what's on the data plate?",o:["It tells you the hazards: toxicity, flammability, what PPE and precautions to use","It doesn't matter. That's for the engineers","It sets the price of the refrigerant","It tells you the charge amount"],a:0,e:"Matching the plate gets the right gas in the system. The class tells you how to stay safe doing it: ignition control and rated tools for an A2L, ventilation and respirator planning for a B, and looking out for whoever is working next to you."},
{t:"oil",q:"About how long can POE oil be open to the air before moisture becomes a problem?",o:["About 15 minutes","About 2 hours","As long as the jug is upright","It doesn't matter, the vacuum fixes it"],a:0,e:"A common rule is no more than about 15 minutes for an open compressor, open piping or an open oil container. POE pulls water from the air fast. Cap lines and plug the compressor between steps. Your compressor maker may give a different number, so check their bulletin.",f:1},
{t:"oil",q:"A tech says, \"Don't worry about the wet POE, I'll just pull a deep vacuum overnight.\" What's wrong with that?",o:["A vacuum pulls free water, but it can't undo water that already reacted with the POE and made acid","Nothing, a deep vacuum dries everything","Vacuum pumps can't be used on POE systems","He should pull a vacuum for only 5 minutes"],a:0,e:"This is a common field belief. A good vacuum does remove free moisture. But water reacts with POE and breaks it down into acid and alcohol, and that can't be pulled back out. Test the oil for acid, change the oil and driers as the maker allows, then retest."},
{t:"oil",q:"Which mix inside a system sets up acid and sludge?",o:["Moisture, air and other contaminants, with heat","Refrigerant and nitrogen","Clean, dry POE and refrigerant","Too much subcooling"],a:0,e:"Moisture plus air (oxygen and non-condensables) plus contaminants like flux or brazing scale, cooked by compressor heat, can set off a chemical reaction. It makes acid that eats motor winding insulation and sludge that plugs metering devices. That's why you braze with nitrogen flowing and keep the system sealed."},
{t:"oil",q:"You suspect a system is acidic, maybe after a burnout. What should you do before deciding on the cleanup?",o:["Test the oil with an acid test kit","Smell the oil and go by that","Add a second filter drier and walk away","Top off with fresh oil"],a:0,e:"An acid test kit tells you for sure. Smell and color can hint at a burnout, but they don't measure acid. The test result decides the cleanup, and you test again after it to prove it worked."},
{t:"oil",type:"multi",q:"The oil in a system tested acidic. Which steps are part of a proper cleanup? Select all that apply.",o:["Change the contaminated oil","Install new filter driers where the maker allows them","Flush the piping where the maker allows it","Retest the oil after running","Pull a longer vacuum and call it fixed","Mix in a different oil type to dilute it"],a:[0,1,2,3],e:"Contaminated oil has to come out and be replaced, with new driers and a flush where the equipment maker allows them, then a retest. A vacuum alone won't remove acid. Never mix oil types. Some equipment, like many VRF systems, doesn't allow field driers or flush chemicals, so check the install manual."},
{t:"basics",q:"R-600a has a bigger number than R-290. Does that tell you R-600a is more flammable?",o:["No. The R-number comes from the chemistry. Flammability is the number in the safety class, like the 3 in A3","Yes, a higher R-number means more flammable","Yes, anything over R-500 is flammable","No, a lower R-number means more flammable"],a:0,e:"Easy to mix up. The R-number is the refrigerant's name, built from its chemistry (the 600s are organics like isobutane). Flammability lives in the safety class: both R-290 and R-600a are A3. R-410A has a high number and is A1. R-32 has a low one and is A2L."},
{t:"fire",q:"A leak in a closet gives an average of 3% refrigerant in the air, well below the LFL. Why could it still light off at the water heater pilot?",o:["The gas pools low, so the air near the floor can be above the LFL even when the room average isn't","The LFL drops as the room gets bigger","Pilot lights lower the LFL","It can't. Below the average LFL nothing can burn"],a:0,e:"The LFL is measured in the lab with an even mix. A real leak isn't even: A2L vapor is heavier than air and pools at the floor, right where pilots and burners sit. That's why you kill ignition sources and get air moving before you open the system."},
{t:"fire",q:"How does ASHRAE decide a refrigerant's flammability class?",o:["Lab testing, using a modified ASTM E681 flask test for flame spread, plus LFL, heat of combustion and burning velocity","Each manufacturer picks the class for its own refrigerant","By the R-number: higher numbers burn more","By how much it costs per pound"],a:0,e:"The class comes from testing, not opinion. A set mix of refrigerant and air in a flask gets a spark, and they watch whether the flame spreads. LFL, heat of combustion and burning velocity then decide 1, 2L, 2 or 3."},
{t:"fire",q:"R-410A is class A1. What does that really mean about flammability?",o:["No flame spread under the standard test conditions (about 140°F, normal air pressure), not that it can never burn","It can never burn under any conditions","It burns slowly like an A2L","It's only flammable in heating mode"],a:0,e:"A1 is a test result. Outside the test conditions, like high pressure mixed with air, some A1 refrigerants can become combustible, and in a flame they all break down into toxic gas. That's why you pressure test with dry nitrogen, never refrigerant and air.",f:1},
{t:"fire",q:"Old ASHRAE class 2 was split into 2 and 2L. What decides which one a refrigerant gets?",o:["Burning velocity: 10 cm/s or less is 2L","Toxicity: 2L is less toxic","GWP: 2L is under 700","The year it was released"],a:0,e:"Both are flammable. If the maximum burning velocity is 10 cm/s or less (about 4 inches a second), it's 2L, mildly flammable. People pick toxicity because of the L, but toxicity is only the letter in front (A or B)."},
{t:"fire",type:"multi",q:"ASHRAE works out the RCL from three limits. Which are they? Select all that apply.",o:["Acute toxicity (ATEL)","Oxygen deprivation (ODL)","Flammability (FCL)","Global warming potential","Operating pressure"],a:[0,1,2],e:"RCL is the lowest of three limits: acute toxicity (ATEL), oxygen deprivation (ODL) and flammability (FCL). The tightest one sets the cap for an occupied room. GWP and pressure don't come into it."},
{t:"fire",q:"For RCL, which of the three limits (toxicity, oxygen, flammability) gets used?",o:["The lowest one, because the tightest limit wins","The highest one","The average of all three","Always the flammability one"],a:0,e:"Whichever limit is reached first is the one that protects people, so the RCL is the lowest of the three. For an A2L that's often the flammability limit, but not always."},
{t:"fire",type:"match",q:"Match each term to what it means.",pairs:[["RCL","Most refrigerant allowed in an occupied room"],["ATEL","Most you can breathe in a short burst before it hurts you"],["IDLH","Immediately dangerous to life or health: get out"],["Flame propagation","The flame spreading out from where it started"]],e:"Memory hooks: RCL = Room's Cap Limit (max occupancy sign). ATEL = too much, too fast. IDLH = I Don't Linger Here. Propagation is what the LFL test watches for."},
{t:"fire",q:"A VRF indoor unit above the ceiling of a small bathroom starts leaking hard when you open the evap fittings. What should you have done first, and do now?",o:["Isolate the unit with the service valves before opening anything. If it gets away, step out and ventilate","Keep working fast to finish before much leaks out","Hold your breath and tighten the fittings","Turn on the bathroom light so you can see better"],a:0,e:"On VRF one indoor unit can draw from a 100+ lb system, and a small room passes its RCL with only a few pounds. Isolate first. If a leak gets away, your safety comes before the charge: get out and get air moving. Light switches can spark, which matters with A2L."},
{t:"fire",q:"Why are most A2L refrigerants hard to ignite compared to propane?",o:["Their minimum ignition energy (MIE) is much higher, and they need more refrigerant in the air before they can burn","They contain no carbon","They're stored at lower pressure","They're heavier than air"],a:0,e:"Propane lights from a tiny spark, a fraction of a millijoule. Most 2Ls need far more spark energy, and their LFL is higher, so more has to leak before the mix can burn at all. Being heavier than air is true, but that makes them pool, not harder to light.",f:1},
{t:"fire",q:"Which refrigerants can make HF and carbonyl fluoride when they burn or hit a glowing heat strip?",o:["Any fluorocarbon: CFCs, HCFCs, HFCs and HFOs, including A1s like R-22 and R-410A","Only A2L refrigerants","Only A3 refrigerants like propane","Only old CFCs like R-12"],a:0,e:"HF comes from the fluorine in the molecule, not from the flammability class. R-410A in a torch flame makes HF just like R-454B does. Propane has no fluorine, so it doesn't make HF, but it's far more flammable."},
{t:"fire",q:"You're working near a heat strip and catch a sharp, stinging smell that burns your nose and throat. What does it most likely mean, and what do you do?",o:["Refrigerant is breaking down into HF or COF2. Get out, ventilate, and get medical attention if anyone breathed it","It's normal dust burning off the heat strip. Keep working","It's the refrigerant's normal smell. Find the leak first","It's the oil. Wipe it down and continue"],a:0,e:"Refrigerant itself has almost no smell. A sharp, acrid, stinging smell near a flame or hot surface is the warning sign of decomposition products like HF. That warning is your cue to leave and air it out, not to finish the job."},
{t:"fire",q:"Why shouldn't you unbraze (sweat out) an old filter drier with a torch?",o:["Heat puts leftover refrigerant and oil in the flame, which can make toxic HF, and it drives the trapped water and acid back into the system","It's faster to cut it, that's the only reason","The torch can't get hot enough","Driers are glued, not brazed"],a:0,e:"The old drier is loaded with water and acid it pulled from the system. Heating it lets that go back into the lines and oil. Any refrigerant or oil left inside can also break down in the flame. Recover, cut it out with a tubing cutter (not a saw, the chips get in), and braze the new one in with nitrogen flowing."},
{t:"fire",type:"multi",q:"Which are the main times a technician could be exposed to toxic refrigerant byproducts or a big release? Select all that apply.",o:["A cylinder leaking inside the service vehicle","A big leak into a small, closed space during service","A leak lighting off at a pilot light or burner","Refrigerant released in a building fire","Reading the nameplate","Charging outdoors on a breezy roof with no ignition sources"],a:[0,1,2,3],e:"Toxic risk is highest in a closed vehicle or a small room. Fire risk shows up when a leak meets an ignition source during operation or service, or in a building fire. Check the refrigerant's SDS for the PPE to use."},
{t:"fire",q:"R-32's auto-ignition temperature is about 1,200°F, and brazing heat runs about 1,050 to 1,600°F. What does that tell you?",o:["Hot copper alone can light R-32 without any spark, so the line must be recovered and purged with nitrogen before brazing","Brazing is always safe on R-32 because the flame burns it off","Auto-ignition only matters for propane","You just need to braze faster"],a:0,e:"Auto-ignition means the mix can light from heat alone, no spark or flame needed. Brazing temperatures reach past that. With A2L, recover the charge, purge with nitrogen, and braze with nitrogen flowing so there's nothing in the pipe to light.",f:1},
{t:"service",q:"Where should brazing flux go when you need it, and when can you skip it?",o:["A thin coat on the outside of the male fitting only. Copper to copper with phos-copper rod doesn't need flux","Pack it inside the fitting so the joint seals","Flux every joint, inside and out","Mix extra water into it so it flows into the pipe"],a:0,e:"Flux has boric acid and fluoride compounds. Inside the system it mixes with moisture and makes acid that attacks POE oil. Use it only where the metals need it (brass, steel, dissimilar metals), thin and on the outside of the male part. Phos-copper rod is self-fluxing on copper to copper."},
];
